\f0\b\fs44 \cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Hidden Architecture of Fiscal Control: The Strategic Convergence of KBLI and KLU in Indonesia\'92s 2025 Regulatory Landscape\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 Executive Summary\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 In the high-stakes arena of Indonesian corporate governance, a profound yet often invisible transformation is reshaping the fundamental relationship between the state and the private sector. For decades, the\'a0
\f0\b Indonesian Standard Industrial Classification (KBLI)
\f1\b0 \'a0was perceived by the business community as a mere administrative formality\'97a bureaucratic code required to obtain a Business Identification Number (NIB) or a trading license. It was a static label, often chosen for convenience rather than precision, and largely detached from the financial realities of the firm. Conversely, the\'a0
\f0\b Tax Classification of Business Fields (KLU)
\f1\b0 \'a0was the exclusive domain of the Directorate General of Taxes (DGT/DJP), a statistical tool used to categorize revenue but rarely synchronized in real-time with the operational licenses issued by other ministries.\cb1 \
\cb3 This era of separation is over. With the enactment of\'a0
\f0\b BPS Regulation No. 7 of 2025
\f1\b0 , which introduces the granular\'a0
\f0\b KBLI 2025
\f1\b0 , and its synchronized integration with the\'a0
\f0\b Coretax Administration System (CTAS)
\f1\b0 , these two classification systems have fused into a singular, high-precision mechanism for fiscal surveillance. The "hidden insight"\'97the\'a0
\f2\i insight nascosto
\f1\i0 \'a0identified by sophisticated tax strategists and legal scholars\'97is that the KBLI code has ceased to be just a licensing designator. It has evolved into the\'a0
\f0\b primary fiscal determinant
\f1\b0 \'a0of a corporate entity.\cb1 \
\cb3 The selection of a KBLI code in the Online Single Submission (OSS) system now acts as the initial input for the DGT's algorithmic\'a0
\f0\b Compliance Risk Management (CRM)
\f1\b0 \'a0engine. It dictates the benchmarked profit margins the government expects, the specific "Norma" (deemed profit rates) applied to revenue, the eligibility for tax incentives like the "Factoryless Goods Producer" status, and the probability of triggering an automated audit.\cb1 \
\cb3 This comprehensive report offers an exhaustive analysis of this convergence. It dissects the structural overhaul of KBLI 2025\'a0, exploring the decoupling of digital content from infrastructure, the formal recognition of the "Green Economy"\'a0, and the automated triangulation of data between the Ministry of Investment (BKPM) and the DGT.\'a0It argues that the "approximate" classifications of the past are now dangerous liabilities. With the critical transition deadline of June 18, 2026, approaching\'a0, businesses operating in Indonesia face a narrowing window to align their operational reality with their regulatory "DNA," or risk being ensnared in a web of automated anomalies and fiscal disputes.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part I: The Anatomy of Classification \'96 From Administration to Intelligence\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 To fully grasp the magnitude of the current regulatory shift, one must first deconstruct the historical architecture of business classification in Indonesia. For years, the Indonesian regulatory environment was characterized by a distinct duality\'97a separation of powers and databases that allowed businesses to maintain slightly different "identities" depending on which government agency was looking. This section explores the traditional separation between licensing and taxation and how the "One Single Submission" (OSS) regime, culminating in the Coretax integration, has systematically eroded these walls to create a unified surveillance apparatus.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 1.1 The Historical Duality: KBLI vs. KLU\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Historically, businesses in Indonesia operated with a dual identity, navigating two parallel but often disconnected systems of classification.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Licensing Identity (KBLI):
\f1\b0 \'a0Managed by the Central Statistics Agency (BPS) and enforced by the Ministry of Investment/Indonesia Investment Coordinating Board (BKPM), the\'a0
\f2\i Klasifikasi Baku Lapangan Usaha Indonesia
\f1\i0 \'a0(KBLI) served as the gatekeeper for market entry. Its primary function was regulatory permission. The KBLI code determined\'a0
\f2\i what
\f1\i0 \'a0a company was legally permitted to do, which specific licenses it required to operate, and critically, its eligibility for foreign investment under the Negative Investment List (DNI) or the Positive Investment List.\'a0For example, a foreign investor might choose a specific KBLI code purely to maximize their allowed equity ownership percentage, even if that code only loosely described their actual business operations.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \

\f0\b \cb3 The Fiscal Identity (KLU):
\f1\b0 \'a0Parallel to this was the\'a0
\f2\i Klasifikasi Lapangan Usaha
\f1\i0 \'a0(KLU), managed exclusively by the Directorate General of Taxes (DGT). While theoretically based on the KBLI structure, the KLU was distinct in its purpose and application. It was used for statistical analysis of tax revenue and, more importantly, for the administration of the "Norma Penghitungan Penghasilan Netto" (Deemed Profit Norms) for individual taxpayers and smaller entities.\'a0The KLU appeared on the Tax ID (NPWP) and was often selected by the taxpayer's finance department or tax consultant based on the activity that generated the most revenue, or in some cases, the activity that offered the most favorable tax treatment.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \

\f0\b \cb3 The Arbitrage of the Silos:
\f1\b0 \'a0For decades, the data silos between the BKPM (Licensing) and the DGT (Tax) allowed a form of regulatory arbitrage to persist. A company might be licensed as a "Management Consultancy" (KBLI 7020) to bypass certain foreign equity caps or capital requirements, but report its taxes as a "Trade Service" or "General Wholesale" (KLU 46xxx) to justify lower operating margins or different VAT treatments. Because the systems did not "speak" to each other in real-time, discrepancies between the activity permitted by the license and the activity reported on the tax return were common, often unnoticed, and rarely punished.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The "hidden insight" that is now emerging is that the government has recognized this gap not just as an administrative inefficiency, but as a major source of revenue leakage. The unification of these codes is the state's strategic response to close this loophole permanently.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 1.2 The Collapse of the Silos: OSS RBA and Coretax\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 The erosion of this duality began with the introduction of the\'a0
\f0\b Risk-Based Online Single Submission (OSS-RBA)
\f1\b0 \'a0system. This platform was the first step in digitizing and centralizing business licensing, linking business activities directly to risk levels (Low, Medium, High) to streamline permit issuance. However, the OSS-RBA was primarily a licensing revolution. The\'a0
\f2\i fiscal
\f1\i0 \'a0revolution arrives with the full implementation of the\'a0
\f0\b Coretax Administration System (CTAS)
\f1\b0 \'a0in 2025/2026.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Under the new Coretax regime, the DGT no longer passively accepts the KLU reported by the taxpayer during annual filing. Instead, the system acts as a centralized data aggregator. It pulls live, real-time data directly from the OSS-RBA database. The implications of this integration are profound and immediate:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Automatic Synchronization:
\f1\b0 \'a0The moment a business updates its license in the OSS system to comply with KBLI 2025\'97perhaps to renew a permit or expand into a new sector\'97that data flows instantly to the DGT. The Coretax system automatically updates the taxpayer's Master File (Masterfile Wajib Pajak), overriding the historical KLU with the new KBLI data.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Legacy Data Cleanup:
\f1\b0 \'a0The transition to Coretax is being utilized as a massive "data cleansing" exercise. The DGT is forcing a reconciliation between the registered activity and the actual revenue streams. Taxpayers are being prompted to validate their NIK (National ID) and NPWP, and in doing so, they are confirming their business activities. Any discrepancy found during this validation phase\'97for example, a company claiming to be inactive while the OSS shows active license renewals\'97triggers an immediate red flag.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Single Source of Truth:
\f1\b0 \'a0The convergence creates a single point of truth for corporate identity. The gap between "what we say we do for the license" and "what we say we do for taxes" is eliminated. If a company is a "Manufacturer" in OSS, it\'a0
\f2\i must
\f1\i0 \'a0be a "Manufacturer" in Coretax, with all the associated tax obligations and manufacturing-level profit expectations.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The transition from siloed databases to the unified Coretax environment creates a single point of truth for corporate identity, effectively eliminating the gap between operational licensing and fiscal reporting. This merger forces businesses to confront the reality that their regulatory classification is now a transparent, immutable declaration of their fiscal profile.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 1.3 The Insight: KBLI as a Proxy for Profitability\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 The most sophisticated aspect of this convergence\'97and the core of the "hidden insight"\'97revolves around the mechanism of\'a0
\f0\b Benchmarking
\f1\b0 . The DGT, despite its modernization, does not possess the manpower to manually audit every single taxpayer in Indonesia's vast economy. Instead, it relies on automation and statistical probability. The KBLI/KLU code is the keystone of this automated oversight.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The DGT uses KLU codes to establish "standard" financial ratios for every specific industry sector. These benchmarks are derived from the aggregated data of all taxpayers within that code, creating a statistical "normal" range for:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gross Profit Margin (GPM)
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Operating Profit Margin (OPM)
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Net Profit Margin (NPM)
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Corporate Tax to Turnover Ratio
\f1\b0 .\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Algorithmic logic:
\f1\b0 \'a0If a company is registered under\'a0
\f0\b KBLI 6202 (Computer Consultancy)
\f1\b0 , the DGT's algorithm expects a financial profile consistent with a service-based consulting firm\'97typically high gross margins (as there is no cost of goods sold in the traditional sense) and a specific range of net profitability (e.g., 15-30%).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Consider a scenario where a company is actually engaged in low-margin hardware trading (buying and selling servers) but has retained the "Computer Consultancy" KBLI code for legacy reasons or licensing ease. Hardware trading typically operates on razor-thin margins (e.g., 2-5%). When this company files its tax return reporting a 4% net profit margin, the Coretax system compares this against the KLU benchmark for "Consultancy" (25%). The system sees a massive "profit gap." It does not "know" the company is actually trading hardware; it only knows the company is\'a0
\f2\i coded
\f1\i0 \'a0as a Consultant but\'a0
\f2\i earning
\f1\i0 \'a0like a Trader.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Consequence:
\f1\b0 \'a0The algorithm flags the company as under-reporting income. This triggers an automated\'a0
\f0\b Request for Explanation (SP2DK)
\f1\b0 , asking the taxpayer to explain the deviation. The burden of proof shifts entirely to the taxpayer to prove they are\'a0
\f2\i not
\f1\i0 \'a0hiding profit, a difficult and costly administrative battle that starts purely because of a KBLI mismatch.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Thus, the KBLI code is not just a label; it is the\'a0
\f0\b denominator
\f1\b0 \'a0in the equation that determines a company's tax compliance score. It sets the bar over which the company must jump every fiscal year.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part II: The Structural Overhaul \'96 KBLI 2025\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 The release of BPS Regulation No. 7 of 2025 introduces the most significant structural overhaul of the classification system in recent history. This is not merely an update or a periodic refresh; it is a fundamental realignment of the Indonesian economy with the\'a0
\f0\b International Standard Industrial Classification of All Economic Activities (ISIC) Revision 5
\f1\b0 .\'a0This alignment ensures that Indonesia's economic data is globally comparable, but for the local business, it means a drastic increase in specificity.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 2.1 The Scope of Change: Granularity as a Tool\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 The new system expands the classification to 22 categories (up from 21 in KBLI 2020), comprising 87 main groups, 257 groups, and a staggering 1,560 specific business activity codes.\'a0This explosion in granularity is designed to eliminate ambiguity, specifically in sectors that were previously shoehorned into "Other" or "General" categories.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The "One-to-Many" Principle:
\f1\b0 \'a0A defining characteristic of KBLI 2025 is the "One-to-Many" principle. Broad codes that covered multiple activities have been shattered into specific niche codes. A general "Software" code in 2020 is now split into specific codes for SaaS, PaaS, bespoke development, and data hosting.\'a0This prevents companies from hiding high-value, specific activities within a generic bucket.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \

\f0\b \cb3 The "Many-to-One" Principle:
\f1\b0 \'a0Conversely, some redundant manufacturing codes have been consolidated ("Many-to-One") to simplify industrial reporting. This impacts the investment thresholds for foreign companies, as consolidated codes might change the "Line of Business" definitions used to calculate minimum capital requirements.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 2.2 The Digital Schism: Categories J and K\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 One of the most critical changes for the modern economy\'97and a focal point for tax authorities\'97is the bifurcation of the Information & Communication sector. Previously a monolith under KBLI 2020, this sector is now split to rigorously differentiate "Content" from "Infrastructure".\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Category J: Content, Media, and the Creator Economy
\f1\b0 \'a0This new category now houses activities related to publishing, broadcasting, and content production/distribution. Crucially, it includes specific codes for the\'a0
\f0\b "Creator Economy"
\f1\b0 \'97YouTubers, influencers, podcasters, and digital content producers.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Fiscal Implication:
\f1\i0 \'a0By creating specific codes for these actors, the DGT can now apply specific "Norma" rates to their revenue. Previously, a YouTuber might have filed as a "Consultant" or "Artist." Now, with a dedicated code, the DGT can track the explosive growth of this sector and apply targeted tax enforcement. It brings the informal digital economy into the formal tax net.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Category K: Telecommunications and Tech Infrastructure
\f1\b0 \'a0This category is strictly reserved for telecommunications, computer programming, consultancy, computing infrastructure, and information services.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Case Study: The Data Center Shift (63112 vs 63102)
\f1\b0 \'a0A prime example of this precision is the treatment of data centers. Under KBLI 2020, data centers often operated under the catch-all\'a0
\f0\b KBLI 63112 (Data Processing)
\f1\b0 .\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Change:
\f1\i0 \'a0KBLI 2025 abolishes 63112 and introduces the specific\'a0
\f0\b KBLI 63102 (Computing Infrastructure, Hosting, and Related Activities)
\f1\b0 , which explicitly covers colocation, cloud solutions (IaaS, PaaS), and dedicated hosting.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Why this matters:
\f1\i0 \'a0"Data Processing" implies a service activity, often asset-light. "Infrastructure/Colocation" implies a capital-intensive, asset-heavy business model.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\i \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tax Consequence:
\f1\i0 \'a0The tax deductibility of depreciation, the expectations for VAT input credits, and the benchmarked Return on Assets (ROA) are fundamentally different for an infrastructure company compared to a data processor. A company failing to migrate to the new 63102 code risks having its depreciation expenses challenged during an audit, as the tax officer's system will still be benchmarking them against asset-light service providers.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 2.3 The "Factoryless" Revolution: IP over Heavy Machinery\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 A nuanced but explosive addition to KBLI 2025 is the formal recognition of\'a0
\f0\b Factoryless Goods Producers (FGPs)
\f1\b0 . This change modernizes the definition of "manufacturing" in Indonesia, aligning it with global supply chains where value is created by Intellectual Property (IP) rather than physical machinery.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Old World Paradigm:
\f1\b0 \'a0Previously, a company that owned a brand (e.g., a local skincare line or electronics brand) but outsourced the actual physical production to a third-party OEM (Original Equipment Manufacturer) was often forced to register as a "Wholesale Trader" (Distributor).\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Consequence:
\f1\i0 \'a0As "Traders," these companies were ineligible for the lucrative tax incentives reserved for "Manufacturers," such as Tax Holidays or Tax Allowances. Furthermore, they faced different VAT dispute risks regarding transfer pricing.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The New World (KBLI 2025):
\f1\b0 \'a0Under the new classification, FGPs can now be classified according to the\'a0
\f2\i product
\f1\i0 \'a0they produce, even if they do not own the factory, provided they own the Intellectual Property (IP) and control the production process.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Opportunity:
\f1\i0 \'a0This opens the door for massive tax optimization. IP-heavy companies can now claim manufacturing status, potentially accessing reduced tax rates and investment incentives.\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\f2\i \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Trap:
\f1\i0 \'a0However, this classification invites intense scrutiny on\'a0
\f0\b Transfer Pricing
\f1\b0 . The DGT will demand robust documentation proving that the "Factoryless Producer" effectively controls the economic risk and creates the value. If a company claims this code but cannot demonstrate substance\'97such as having R&D staff, quality control oversight, or IP ownership in Indonesia\'97the DGT will reclassify them as a simple trader, retroactively revoking incentives and imposing penalties.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part III: The "Hidden" Fiscal Weapon \'96 Compliance Risk Management (CRM)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 The convergence of KBLI and KLU feeds directly into the DGT\'92s master engine: the\'a0
\f0\b Compliance Risk Management (CRM)
\f1\b0 \'a0system. This is the "brain" of the modern Indonesian tax authority, an automated system that profiles taxpayers and selects them for audit without initial human intervention.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 3.1 The CRM Matrix: The Four Quadrants of Exposure\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 The CRM system utilizes a risk matrix that places every taxpayer into one of four quadrants based on two primary axes:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Axis X: Probability of Non-Compliance.
\f1\b0 \'a0This score is derived from historical behavior (late filings, previous amendments) and, crucially,\'a0
\f2\i data mismatches
\f1\i0 \'a0between reported figures and third-party data (banks, customs, OSS).\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Axis Y: Consequence of Non-Compliance.
\f1\b0 \'a0This measures the\'a0
\f2\i revenue magnitude
\f1\i0 \'a0and the\'a0
\f2\i potential tax gap
\f1\i0 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The "Hidden Insight" here is that the\'a0
\f0\b KLU is the primary variable for calculating the 'Potential Tax Gap'
\f1\b0 \'a0on the Y-axis. The system calculates the gap by comparing the taxpayer's actual tax payment against the "Potential" payment expected from a typical company with that specific KLU.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 3.2 The Mechanism of Automated Profiling\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 When a taxpayer files their Annual Tax Return (SPT), the Coretax system performs an instantaneous, invisible cross-check:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Identify KLU:
\f1\b0 \'a0The system pulls the KLU (synced from the KBLI in OSS).\cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Retrieve Benchmark:
\f1\b0 \'a0It retrieves the "Industry Standard" ratios for that KLU. For example, the system knows that a "Management Consulting" firm (KBLI 7020) typically operates with a Net Profit Margin (NPM) of 15-25% because it has low overheads.\cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Compare Actuals:
\f1\b0 \'a0The system looks at the filed NPM.\cb1 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl1
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Scenario:
\f1\i0 \'a0The taxpayer reports an NPM of only 4%.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Deviation Flag:
\f1\b0 \'a0The gap (25% benchmark vs. 4% actual) is statistically significant.\cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Trigger:
\f1\b0 \'a0Because the deviation is high, the CRM score spikes into the "High Risk" quadrant. The system automatically generates an\'a0
\f0\b SP2DK (Letter of Request for Explanation)
\f1\b0 \'a0asking the taxpayer to explain the discrepancy. The question is essentially:\'a0
\f2\i "Why is your profitability 80% lower than the industry average for KLU 7020?"
\f1\i0 .\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 3.3 The Trap of Generic Codes\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 A common practice among businesses, often born of uncertainty or laziness during registration, is to dump activities into "Generic" codes or "Other Business Activities Not Elsewhere Classified" (n.e.c.).\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Risk:
\f1\b0 \'a0Generic codes are often assigned the\'a0
\f2\i highest
\f1\i0 \'a0risk profiles by the DGT. Historically, these codes have been used to obscure business activities and facilitate tax avoidance.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Consequence:
\f1\b0 \'a0A business that uses a generic "Trading" code because it was the "easiest" option during incorporation might find itself benchmarked against the most profitable traders in the country. They create a perpetual struggle to justify their "underperformance" against a vague and high-bar benchmark, leading to repeated cycles of SP2DKs and potential audits.\'a0We can visualize a risk matrix where generic trading codes and digital intermediaries occupy the high-risk/low-complexity quadrant, subjecting them to intense algorithmic scrutiny compared to specifically coded, compliant manufacturers.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part IV: The Strategic Trap \'96 Commercial Reality vs. Regulatory Coding\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 The danger of KBLI 2025 lies in the\'a0
\f0\b mismatch
\f1\b0 . A mismatch occurs when the commercial reality of a business evolves\'97pivoting to new products, adopting new business models\'97but its regulatory "DNA" (KBLI) remains static. In the past, this was an administrative oversight; today, it is a "structural problem" that triggers automated enforcement.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 4.1 The "Drift" Phenomenon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 As companies grow, they naturally pivot. A software development house (originally Category K) might begin generating significant revenue from ad revenue on a media blog it developed (Category J) or from selling branded merchandise (Trade Category).\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In OSS:
\f1\b0 \'a0This drift is often ignored. The company continues to renew its existing license without adding new KBLI codes for the new revenue streams.\cb1 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In Coretax:
\f1\b0 \'a0This drift is fatal. If the revenue stream (e.g., ad revenue) has a different VAT treatment or withholding tax obligation than the registered KBLI, the system flags a "VAT Gap."\cb1 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl1
\f2\i \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Example:
\f1\i0 \'a0Selling merchandise requires inventory tracking, logistics costs, and warehousing. Selling software does not. If a "Software" company suddenly reports high logistics costs in its financial statements (because they are actually selling merchandise), the CRM system flags the expense as "Anomalous." Why would a software consultancy have high freight bills? This anomaly triggers an audit to investigate potential inflated expenses.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 4.2 The Case of the "Digital Intermediary"\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 KBLI 2025 introduces specific codes for\'a0
\f0\b Digital Platforms (Intermediaries)
\f1\b0 . This targets marketplaces, ride-hailing apps, and online health consultation services.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Conflict:
\f1\b0 \'a0Many platforms previously registered simply as "IT Companies" or "Software Developers."\cb1 \
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Tax Implication:
\f1\b0 \'a0An IT company provides a service (subject to Withholding Tax Art 23). A Digital Intermediary might be considered a "Trader" or a "Marketplace" with obligations to collect VAT (PMSE) and withhold tax on behalf of sellers.\cb1 \
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The PMK 37/2025 Trap:
\f1\b0 \'a0The new regulation\'a0
\f0\b PMK 37/2025
\f1\b0 \'a0mandates that online marketplace platforms withhold Article 22 Income Tax on income earned by domestic sellers.\'a0If a platform remains registered as an IT service provider but operates as a marketplace, they are arguably failing to withhold these taxes for their merchants.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Nightmare Scenario:
\f1\b0 \'a0The DGT can calculate the\'a0
\f2\i total transaction value
\f1\i0 \'a0(Gross Merchandise Value - GMV) flowing through the platform and, due to the misclassification, assess it as the company's own revenue rather than just the commission. This leads to a massive, albeit factually incorrect, tax bill that the company must fight to disprove in tax court\'97all because they failed to adopt the correct "Digital Intermediary" KBLI code.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part V: The Green Economy & Carbon Classification\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 Beyond the digital realm, KBLI 2025 positions Indonesia to monetize the "Green Economy." The introduction of specific codes for Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) is a strategic move to formalize the carbon market and prepare for future carbon taxation.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 5.1 Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) Codes\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 KBLI 2025 introduces two critical codes that were previously non-existent:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 KBLI 39001 (Carbon Capture Activities):
\f1\b0 \'a0Covering services that prevent CO2 emissions through capture technologies (pre/post-combustion, direct air capture).\cb1 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 KBLI 39002 (Carbon Storage Activities):
\f1\b0 \'a0Covering the geological storage of carbon, such as in depleted oil fields or saline aquifers.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Fiscal Strategy:
\f1\b0 \'a0Indonesia is positioning itself as a regional hub for Carbon Storage (CCS/CCUS). These codes are the precursors to a\'a0
\f0\b Carbon Tax
\f1\b0 \'a0regime. By forcing companies to register under these specific codes, the government identifies the exact population of taxpayers liable for future carbon levies or eligible for carbon credit (Nilai Ekonomi Karbon) trading.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Risk:
\f1\b0 \'a0A company engaging in carbon storage activities without the proper code (perhaps hiding it under "General Mining Support Services") risks being accused of avoiding specific carbon levies. Conversely, correctly registering allows the company to participate in the formal carbon credit exchange, but it also flags them for immediate scrutiny regarding their environmental compliance and carbon accounting.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part VI: The Audit Mechanism \'96 From Code to SP2DK\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 The SP2DK (
\f2\i Surat Permintaan Penjelasan atas Data dan/atau Keterangan
\f1\i0 ) is the weapon of choice for the modern Indonesian tax officer. It is not a full audit; it is a "pre-audit" inquiry. However, how one responds to an SP2DK determines whether the file is closed or escalated to a full\'a0
\f0\b Pemeriksaan (Tax Audit)
\f1\b0 .\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 6.1 The Automated Trigger Hierarchy\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Under the Coretax system, SP2DKs are increasingly automated based on KBLI/KLU logic:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Trigger 1: The Margin Mismatch.
\f1\b0 \'a0(As discussed in Part 3).\cb1 \
\ls14\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Trigger 2: The Input-Output VAT Ratio.
\f1\b0 \'a0The DGT has benchmarks for how much Input VAT (Purchase Tax) a specific KLU should have relative to Output VAT (Sales Tax). If a "Consultancy" firm (KBLI 7020) claims massive Input VAT refunds\'97behavior typical of manufacturing or trading\'97the system flags it as suspicious. The logic is: "Consultants sell time; they don't buy enough physical goods to generate this much refundable VAT".\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\ls14\ilvl0
\f0\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Trigger 3: The Cross-Border Payment.
\f1\b0 \'a0If a KLU indicates a purely domestic business (e.g., Local Retail), but the Coretax system detects significant offshore payments (via withheld Article 26 tax), it suggests an undeclared business line, a hidden royalty scheme, or profit shifting to an offshore entity.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 6.2 The "Concrete Data" Regulation (PER-18/PJ/2025)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb3 The DGT has recently tightened the definition of "Concrete Data" that can trigger an audit.\'a0
\f0\b Regulation PER-18/PJ/2025
\f1\b0 \'a0specifies that "Concrete Data" includes data from tax invoices approved but not reported, and withholding tax slips not reported.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Link to KBLI:
\f1\b0 \'a0If a business receives income that implies a different KBLI (e.g., a "Construction" withholding slip received by a "Trading" company), this is now treated as "Concrete Data" of a misclassification. This justifies an immediate\'a0
\f0\b Specific Tax Audit (Pemeriksaan Spesifik)
\f1\b0 , bypassing the usual polite inquiry stage.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Part VII: Strategic Remediation & The June 2026 Deadline\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 The "Doomsday Clock" for this convergence is\'a0
\f0\b June 18, 2026
\f1\b0 .\'a0This is the hard deadline for all businesses to align their OSS data with KBLI 2025.\'a0\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 7.1 The Transition Timeline\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
